## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `ThreadAbortException`, `Thread.ResetAbort()` — all gone. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

### Pattern classification

| Usage in your code | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or `Join(ms)` |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token))
        {
            IsBackground = true
        };
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort equivalent needed. Just decide: break or continue.
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal the token — cooperative, not forcible

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop in time. You CANNOT force-kill it.
            // Log a warning — see pitfalls below.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key changes explained

1. **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — the token is thread-safe by design and can also unblock waiting calls.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — signals the token; the loop checks `IsCancellationRequested` cooperatively.

3. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`** — the `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not someone else's.

4. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** — no equivalent needed. After catching `OperationCanceledException`, execution continues normally (no auto-rethrow like `ThreadAbortException` had).

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(2s)`** — `Join` actually waits for the thread rather than sleeping blindly.

6. **`IDisposable` added** — `CancellationTokenSource` is disposable; leaking it leaks timers and callbacks.

### Critical pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | If it doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`, cancellation stalls there. You *must* thread the token through to any blocking I/O or `WaitHandle`. |
| **No force-kill fallback** | There is **no** equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in .NET 8. If the thread won't cooperate, it won't stop. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top-level loop. Don't catch and swallow it in `ProcessItem` — let it propagate up. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Causes memory/timer leaks. Always `Dispose()` it. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint (`ThrowIfCancellationRequested`) or cancellation-aware API call. Long gaps between checks = delayed shutdown. |

### If `GetNextItem` is a blocking queue

A common sub-pattern — here's how to make it cancellable:

```csharp
// .NET Framework (blocks forever)
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// .NET 8 (cancellable)
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) throws
// OperationCanceledException when the token fires.
```
